{ https://youtu.be/W0UmYoFigy4?si=dg5dQX7JZgDoh30c&t=97 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } g6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. f4 { [%clk 0:04:52] } (3. Bd3 e5 { If we played Bd3 here it would allow e5. }) 3... Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:50] Here Aman sets up the pawns first. Later in the speed run he will prioritize LSB development and play almost exclusively 3. Bd3. However, if our opponent plays Nc6 it forces us to play f4 in order to stop e5. } d5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 6. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:45] } h6 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 7. Bxf5 { [%clk 0:04:38] Here Aman trades the DSB. In later games he will almost always avoid doing this. } (7. Qc2 e6 8. Nbd2 { In later games Aman will put more focus on controlling e4 to prevent Ne4 from our opponent. }) 7... gxf5 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:32] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 9. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:27] The LSB maneuver. One trade off in the stonewall is that our LSB is terrible. It can become a powerful piece later in the game, especially if we accomplish playing e4. It can also be a nice defender, especially as several opponents will bring their queen to the queen side to target the b pawn (as that is a typical plan in the London maybe they assume it will work against our d4 opening).
This maneuver tends to be more of a plan b/c later in the speed run, but here Aman highlights how with 3 tempi we can move it Bd7, Be8, Bh4 and activate it outside of the pawn chain. } Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 10. h3 { [%clk 0:04:24] kicking out the knight. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 11. Be1 { [%clk 0:04:20] } h5 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 12. Bh4 { [%clk 0:04:18] Pinning the knight. } Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:13] } 13. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:08] The thematic Ne5 jump. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 14. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:00] We almost always take with the f pawn here. Opens up our f file for the rook. Gives us a "lance pawn" on e5 which closes off the king side and gives us opportunity to coordinate our pieces here while our opponent is limited in mobilizing their pieces for defense.
Note that this also pressures the pinned knight, meaning we will win a piece here. It is common to win a piece here at lower elos as often we get a fork when our opponent has knight bishop and knight on the d6, f6, and, say, c6 squares and captures our knight with their c6 knight, for example. } Ne4?? { [%clk 0:04:05] dropping the queen. } 15. Bxd8! { [%clk 0:03:55] } Kxd8 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 16. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:53] Developing and looking to trade down to a winning end game. } Bh6 { [%clk 0:03:52] } 17. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:03:50] } dxe4 { [%clk 0:03:46] } 18. Qe2 { [%clk 0:03:42] } (18. Qxh5?? Bxe3+ 19. Kh2 Rxh5 { There's always a way to capture defeat from the jaws of victory. }) 18... f4 { [%clk 0:03:26] } 19. exf4 { [%clk 0:03:33] } Rg8 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 20. Qxh5 { [%clk 0:03:30] Now we can take this h pawn as Be3+ is not available. } Rg3 { [%clk 0:03:08] } 21. Qxh6 { [%clk 0:03:23] "Technically Qh4+ is the better move" according to Aman. (winning the rook). But the engine actually says taking the bishop is best here.
If the king doesn't move Qh4+ or Qh1+ next to pick up a rook. } (21. Qh4+ Kc8 22. Qxg3 { winning the rook. }) 21... Kd7 { [%clk 0:03:02] evading our fork ideas. } 22. f5 { [%clk 0:03:17] } exf5 { [%clk 0:02:59] } 23. Rxf5 { [%clk 0:03:14] } Rag8 { [%clk 0:02:57] } 24. Rxf7+ { [%clk 0:03:11] } Kc8 { [%clk 0:02:54] } 25. Qe6+ { [%clk 0:03:07] } Kb8 { [%clk 0:02:50] } 26. Re7 { [%clk 0:03:05] Allow him hope with Rxg2+ but it's easily dealt with. } Rxg2+ { [%clk 0:02:47] } 27. Kh1 { [%clk 0:03:02] without the rook on a8 we would be checkmated here. Actually this rook is often left unused in the corner there, especially when things go as planned with the king side attack and you can often checkmate without even needing it, especially at low ELOs. } R2g7 { [%clk 0:02:39] } 28. Re8+ { [%clk 0:03:00] } Rxe8 { [%clk 0:02:35] } 29. Qxe8# { [%clk 0:02:59] and our opponent is punishing for having no snork (escape square) for his king. } 1-0

