{ https://youtu.be/W0UmYoFigy4?si=5XbljW0sn910A-CW&t=1058 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2. f4 { [%clk 0:04:55] Aman is experimenting with the move order here. Later we'll play f4 when e4 is threatened. For example, after Nc6. But generally we'll play d4... e3... Bd3 as our first 3 moves and the order of our f4 Nd2 Nf3 O-O c3 moves will depend on what our opponent plays. } Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:49] } Bb4+ { [%clk 0:04:44] we always love to see this move. } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:47] Playing c3 with tempo is a great feeling. } Ba5 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 6. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:45] And pre-moving recapture with the queen. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:41] } Nh6 { [%clk 0:04:11] Aman mentions around here that "we can definitely take Bxf5" but later in the series he says we never want to take it. He is finding his feet with the opening, especially in the 400-500 elo range, and so we see more experimentation here and ideas floated which are discarded once the speed run really gets going from 500 elo+ } 8. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:31] And we have achieved our primary goals of getting a knight on e5 and keeping our opponent's knight out of e4. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:03] } 9. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:29] Everything going to plan. We have that beautiful lance pawn on e5. It is going to make it very difficult for our opponent to get his pieces across and coordinated to defend his king side. On the other hand we have king side space and all of our pieces pointed in that direction. } Bg4 { [%clk 0:03:58] } (9... O-O 10. Bxf5 Nxf5 11. Qf3 c6 12. Nd2 Bc7 13. e4 { This e4 pawn break is a strong plan b we have in our pocket, unleashing the DSB. } dxe4 14. Nxe4 { And from here, Nf6 sack ideas, g4 ideas to attack the knight Bg5 ideas to attack the queen. }) 10. Qe1 { [%clk 0:04:11] Achieving a move we want to do anyway. Qe1 is a common idea in the stonewall. From there we access the h file via h4 or the g file via g3.
The other common move for our queen is Qf3 following the Ne5 jump. } (10. Qa4+ c6 { blocks the check and defends the bishop with the queen. Generally we should not be playing on the queen side in the stonewall. As taught by Silman in Amateur's Mind, we should be playing on the side where our pawn chain is pointing, i.e. the side where we have more space. That is the king side. There will be times in this speed run Aman even forgoes winning free pawns when they are on the queenside. We don't want to be distracted from our main mission, which is building up a kingside assault. }) 10... f5 { [%clk 0:03:53] } 11. exf6 { [%clk 0:04:00] we generally take en passant here. } gxf6 { [%clk 0:03:51] allowing our queen access to h4. } 12. Qh4 { [%clk 0:03:54] Hitting the knight, which is also defending the bishop. } Nf5?? { [%clk 0:03:44] drops the bishop. Engine wanted Bf5, which would have at least threatened our bishop so on Qxh6, Bxd3. } (12... Bf5 13. Qxh6 Bxd3) 13. Qxg4 { [%clk 0:03:50] and we are already winning. } Qd6 { [%clk 0:03:28] } 14. Bxf5 { [%clk 0:03:38] Looking to trade down to a winning end game. } e5 { [%clk 0:03:20] } 15. Qh5+ { [%clk 0:03:27] Qg7 was also an option. } Ke7 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 16. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:21] Continuing development. Aman is keeping an eye on the f5 square as a square to land the knight for a royal fork. } Rag8 { [%clk 0:03:06] } 17. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:18] } b5 { [%clk 0:02:57] } 18. Bc2 { [%clk 0:03:06] the Dark Magician Hambo with an inconspicuous Bc2. Nh4... Nf5 planned. } h6 { [%clk 0:02:48] } 19. Nh4 { [%clk 0:03:03] } Rg5 { [%clk 0:02:42] Our opponent foils us! } 20. Nf5+ { [%clk 0:02:59] But still we are happy to win an exchange here. } Ke6 { [%clk 0:02:34] Good move by our opponent, keeping pieces on the board and creating complications while down material is the best way to create practical winning chances. } 21. Qh3 { [%clk 0:02:49] } Qc6 { [%clk 0:02:27] } 22. e4 { [%clk 0:02:42] discovered attack on the rook. } Kd7 { [%clk 0:02:15] } 23. Bxg5 { [%clk 0:02:39] } fxg5 { [%clk 0:02:11] } 24. dxe5 { [%clk 0:02:34] } Re8 { [%clk 0:02:08] } 25. Nd4+ { [%clk 0:02:31] discovered check. } Ke7 { [%clk 0:01:51] hangs mate in one. } 26. Nxc6# { [%clk 0:02:30] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

