{ https://youtu.be/W0UmYoFigy4?si=0m_Hlgp5yfUY8h2A&t=1504 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. f4 { [%clk 0:04:57] Again, e3 will be the most common second move as we get further into the speed run. f4 immediately like this gives our opponent too much information about our intentions. } c5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:04:28] An important moment. In the stonewall we want to achieve a couple of things relevant to our opponent's threat here. 1: We want to minimize queenside counterplay, so we aim to keep the c file closed, and our opponent's knight out of b4. 2: We want to get rid of our e pawn if possible, which is a weakness in the stonewall structure. This gets rid of a weakness and also opens up our DSB, especially after we recapture our stonewall knight with our f pawn and then the c1-h6 diagonal fully clears. Therefore we play e6 here, not c6.
Later in the speed run we will play e6 earlier, so this exact scenario will be less of an issue. But there will be cases of opponent's putting pressure on our f and d pawns which will force us into some compromises. For now, we need to know the plan of trading the e pawn off if possible and keeping the c file closed. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:25] We get the chance to follow up with c3 here, but again, it's important to understand that we plan to recapture with the e pawn should cxd4 happen. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:35] threatening Ne4 } 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:11] An early Bd3 is good especially if our opponent has not developed their bishop to this diagonal. As the speed run progresses we will play 1. d4 2. e6 3. Bd3 as our first three moves. } g6 { [%clk 0:04:24] } 6. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:09] } c4 { [%clk 0:04:16] we love to see this move. We can just withdraw our bishop and this locks up the queenside and it makes it easier to play an e4 break later on. } 7. Bc2 { [%clk 0:04:04] } Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:15] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:02] } Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 9. Bxf5 { [%clk 0:03:52] Here Aman takes Bxf5. Later in the speed run he'll go out of his way to avoid this. } (9. Nbd2 Bxc2 10. Qxc2 { Later on Aman will put far more priority on controlling e4 to prevent Ne4. }) 9... gxf5 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 10. Bd2 { [%clk 0:03:48] beginning the DSB maneuver. } Qd6 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 11. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:28] Now the DSB maneuver is no longer as appealing as we will no longer be pinning the knight to the queen when we arrive on h4. Also, the configuration of our opponent's pieces around the e5 square are such that they will plunder a fork on Nxe5. So we prioritize our Ne5 jump here. Later in the speed run we will almost always favor the Ne5 jump as plan A. } Ne4 { [%clk 0:02:59] Allowing Ne4. Aman will go to great lengths to avoid this as the elo of our opponent's increases. } 12. Be1 { [%clk 0:03:23] continuing the DSB maneuver. Later in the speed run Aman will go to far greater lengths to prevent our opponent playing Ne4. This includes no trading off the LSB and bringing the b1 knight out to d2 far sooner. } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:02:46] } 13. fxe5 { [%clk 0:03:21] } Qg6 { [%clk 0:02:41] } 14. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:15] trying to remove what is probably the strongest piece in our opponent's position. } Rg8 { [%clk 0:02:34] } 15. Qf3 { [%clk 0:03:10] building up pressure on the knight, which would be threatening to win a pawn, but alas Aman traded the LSB earlier gifting our opponent the stonewall pawn structure, locking that knight in.
In this case hitting the f pawn and also defends the g2 square.
Later in the speed run Aman will be a lot more concerned with not allowing our opponent to get the stonewall pawn structure against us. } O-O-O { [%clk 0:02:17] } 16. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:03:07] Choosing to eliminate the strong knight over winning the f pawn. (the f pawn can be won because it's taken with check. Otherwise we would blunder mate). } fxe4 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 17. Qf5+ { [%clk 0:03:03] forcing a queen trade. } Qxf5 { [%clk 0:01:08] } 18. Rxf5 { [%clk 0:03:02] } e6 { [%clk 0:01:07] drops the f pawn. } 19. Rxf7 { [%clk 0:02:59] Bh4.... Raf1 ideas } Rd7 { [%clk 0:00:48] saves the h7 pawn. } 20. Rxd7 { [%clk 0:02:52] } Kxd7 { [%clk 0:00:46] } 21. Bh4 { [%clk 0:02:52] we greatly improve our DSB when we get it to h4. } Ne7 { [%clk 0:00:24] } 22. Bxe7 { [%clk 0:02:48] } Kxe7 { [%clk 0:00:22] } 23. Rf1 { [%clk 0:02:47] } h5 { [%clk 0:00:13] } 24. Rf4 { [%clk 0:02:42] } b5 { [%clk 0:00:02] } 25. a3 { [%clk 0:02:39] } a5 { [%clk 0:00:01] } 26. g3 { [%clk 0:02:35] 1-0 White wins. "getting all our pawns on dark squares" -- I don't think due to any positional considerations, but probably just aesthetic ones, as our opponent is running out of time and we only need to play waiting moves to flag them. } 1-0

