{ https://youtu.be/DYjp2MrfxkA?si=i_hJ6Rmfv9NptWlg&t=11 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } c6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:52] Here we see Aman adjust move order from the second video in the Stonewall speed run series, the 500-600 elo one. We will be playing these three moves first in pretty much every game going forward. } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:55] We love to see this move. But we are not playing a London and our DSB will not move for some time, so have at it attacking the b2 pawn, bud. } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:46] we now have lots of time to play f4 and castle. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 5. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:33] stopping Ne4 } Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 6. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:31] We were planning to play this anyway. } Bxf3 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 7. Nxf3 { [%clk 0:04:20] Qxf3 was also an option. It's a trade off between us getting in Ne5 and allowing our opponent to get in Ne4. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:19] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 9. c3 { [%clk 0:04:17] Completing our set up. The only downside to our position is we can't keep his knight out of e4. (well, we can, only it'll be a trade for our LSB as we can still always snap it off before our opponent can play f5. If they know what they are doing they play Nd7... f5... then Nf6... Ne5 but that is costly in terms of tempi. ) } O-O { [%clk 0:04:28] } (9... Nfd7 10. Ne5 f5 11. Qh5+ g6 12. Nxg6 hxg6 13. Qxh8+ { An example of an opponent trying to set up a stonewall and get their knight locked on e4, but not castling and falling for tactics. }) 10. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:16] We can play this without thinking. We did the hard work to achieve our set up and now it's time to fire the stonewall into offensive action. } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 11. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:14] depending on where the knight moves, this could get real ugly, real fast. } Nfd7 { [%clk 0:03:57] } 12. Qh5 { [%clk 0:04:02] our queen often ends up on h5 if our opponent allows it. Threatens mate in one which is going to force our opponent to weaken their pawns. } g6 { [%clk 0:03:50] forced. } 13. Qh6 { [%clk 0:03:57] with DSB maneuver ideas to follow. } Re8 { [%clk 0:03:48] this rook move should set the spidey senses tingling for Rxf7. That e5 lance pawn is cutting off the king's escape via d6. Our opponents pieces are stranded on the queen side. All of our pieces are aimed at the kingside and can be brought to bear within 1, 2, or 3 tempi. } 14. Rxf7 { [%clk 0:03:50] sacking the rook but it's mate in 5 from here. } Kxf7 { [%clk 0:03:45] } 15. Qxh7+ { [%clk 0:03:47] } Kf8 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 16. Bxg6 { [%clk 0:03:41] here e4 followed by brining in the DSB, rook, all winning. Bxg6 is good because on the natural Re7 we have mate in one. } Re7 { [%clk 0:03:00] } (16... Nxe5 17. dxe5 Qc7 18. Qh8+ Ke7 19. Qxe8# { Best defense still leads to mate. }) 17. Qh8# { [%clk 0:03:39] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

