{ https://youtu.be/DYjp2MrfxkA?si=D2WlQDQxLpxgb7-V&t=612 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:58] Not threatening e5 } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:56] we have time to play our preferred move order here as e5 is not threatened. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:53] We love to see this move (blocking in the LSB). } 4. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:52] We don't want to deal with Ne4 so we play this here. } c5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:50] On cxd4 we will recapture with exd4 and get rid of our weak e pawn. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 6. f4 { [%clk 0:04:49] it's best to play this as late as possible as it clearly tips off our opponent we are trying to get a stonewall set up. But now we have our pieces in place to deny Ne4 the opponent will struggle to copy cat us. } cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 7. exd4 { [%clk 0:04:47] We always want to recapture this. It's not always possible (sometimes we can't leave defense of the f pawn) but it's always what we want to do. } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 8. g3 { [%clk 0:04:05] Defending the f pawn. Another option was Ndf3 which would discover the DSB as a defender of the f pawn but we want to leave this knight here if possible to defend the e4 square. } b6 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 9. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:03] preparing Ne5. } a6 { [%clk 0:03:34] } (9... Bb7 { If our opponent had played Bb7 here, further adding pressure to the e4 square, then likely his next move would be Ne7 adding pressure to the e4 square, enabling Ne4 followed by f5. So to stop this we would look to play Qe2 to add further control over the e4 square. } 10. Ne5 (10. Qe2) 10... Ne7 11. O-O Ne4 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. Bxe4 Bxe4) 10. O-O { [%clk 0:04:01] } b5 { [%clk 0:03:33] } 11. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:59] } Bxe5? { [%clk 0:03:21] this is already a mistake, even a blunder according to the engine. } 12. fxe5 { [%clk 0:03:59] } Ne4? { [%clk 0:03:19] This move is not available to him. } 13. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:03:23] Aman thinks either Nxe4 or Bxe4 is crushing. But in this case he chooses Bxe4 because he thinks keeping the knight and heading to d6 for example is more likely to lead to checkmate. } dxe4 { [%clk 0:03:18] } 14. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:03:23] We are threatening Nd6+ so our opponent is pretty much forced to castle here. } Bb7? { [%clk 0:03:10] Allowing Nd6+ } 15. Nd6+ { [%clk 0:03:19] } Ke7?? { [%clk 0:02:57] blundering mate in 1. } (15... Qxd6 16. exd6 { sacking the queen was needed to avoid mate. }) 16. Rxf7# { [%clk 0:03:17] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

