{ https://youtu.be/DYjp2MrfxkA?si=pRHAnj5BKuXM71Na&t=877 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:54] preventing e5. On Nb4, we will probably play Be2 to save our bishop, before playing c3 to boot the knight, and then Bd3 to put the bishop back. } f5 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:48] Not sure why Aman favors this instead of playing c3 to keep the knight out of b4. Not playing Nd2 is understandable since the opponent has mirrored our stonewall pawn structure so it will be difficult to keep his knight out of e4. We don't try to stop Ne4 because we can't now the opponent has achieved the symmetrical stonewall. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:46] again not sure why c3 not played. } (6. c3) 6... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 7. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:44] usually we play c3 and Nd2 to prepare this jump. I understand not playing Nd2 but why doesn't Aman play c3? Perhaps he wanted to demonstrate moving the bishop before kicking the knight. } Nxe5?? { [%clk 0:04:17] blundering a piece. } 8. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:43] } Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 9. exd6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } Nxe3?? { [%clk 0:04:07] dropping another piece. } 10. Bxe3 { [%clk 0:04:37] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

