{ https://youtu.be/DYjp2MrfxkA?si=bQjW_xJXcJ68gw7p&t=1791 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. f4 { [%clk 0:04:54] Preventing e5 } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 4. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:52] Discouraging Ne4 and claiming the diagonal before our opponent. } Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Bxf3 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 6. Qxf3 { [%clk 0:04:46] If our knight was developed we would have likely recaptured Nxf3. If we were castled then Rxf3 would also have been an option. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 7. c3 { [%clk 0:04:45] Solidifying the pawn structure and keeping our opponent's knight out of b4 We can delay Nd2 because we already have two defenders of the e4 square with the queen on f3. } g6 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:43] } Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 9. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:39] starting our DSB maneuver "plan B" now that we don't immediately have our "Plan A" of Ne5. } O-O { [%clk 0:04:27] } 10. Be1 { [%clk 0:04:38] } Qd6 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 11. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:35] No rush to play Bh4 since it wouldn't pin the knight now, so we patiently develop our knight.
We want to get this knight to e5 but we have some work to do to get it there.
We don't want to do something like Qe2... Nf3 trying to get Ne5 only to see our opponent play Ne4... f5. } Rae8 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 12. Bh4 { [%clk 0:04:33] completing our maneuver. joining our rooks. } h6 { [%clk 0:03:41] } 13. Rae1 { [%clk 0:04:20] Just patiently improving the position. It is important to see that g5 is not a threat here. On g5, fxg5, hxg5, Bxg5 and we still have the pressure on the knight but now with added pressure from the queen threatening to win a clean piece. Also opens up the h file towards our opponent's king.
In the post game analysis, Aman mentioned that his plan here (before the opponent went on to blunder) was to play Qh3 followed by Nf3... Ne5. } g5?? { [%clk 0:03:39] Looks like it might trap the bishop but actually blunders a piece and opens up the h file for checkmate ideas. } 14. fxg5 { [%clk 0:04:15] } hxg5 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 15. Bxg5 { [%clk 0:04:14] } b6 { [%clk 0:02:56] } 16. Bxf6 { [%clk 0:04:04] } Bxf6 { [%clk 0:02:53] } 17. Qh5 { [%clk 0:03:24] we have bigger ambitions than winning a bishop. } (17. Qg4+ Bg7 { we don't check first because it allows the bishop to block check and frees up the f pawn to move. }) 17... Rb8 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 18. Qh7# { [%clk 0:03:15] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

