{ https://youtu.be/6yYOQWfQwCk?si=A6d7-vJPJnrkRfjg&t=480 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. f4 { [%clk 0:04:55] stopping e5 } Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:53] rare move order. Usually we will see Bd3 here, and even c3 and Nd2 before Nf3. Possibly we are not in such a rush to play Bd3 here because our opponent has already claimed the diagonal. But later on we will be more likely to immediately challenge the bishop and fight for the diagonal. } (4. Bd3 e6 5. c3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 Qh4+ 7. g3 Qg4 8. Nd2 { Supporting Ngf3 } Nf6 9. Ngf3 Bd6 10. O-O O-O 11. Ne5 Qh5 12. e4 dxe4 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Qxe4 f6 15. Nxc6 bxc6 16. Qxc6 { A possible alternative continuation }) 4... e6 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Bxd3 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 6. Qxd3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Nb4 { [%clk 0:04:43] wildly annoying but no big deal. We'll just move our queen away and focus on castling. } 7. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:46] } b5 { [%clk 0:04:30] a free pawn. } 8. c3 { [%clk 0:04:36] we don't pawn grab, especially not on the queenside. The stonewall is all about kingside attack. } c6 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 9. O-O { [%clk 0:04:26] Not pawn grabbing on the queenside makes sense, but ignoring free pieces is probably a bit much. Aman sticking with the Stonewall standard moves as much as possible for the sake of the content here. } Na6 { [%clk 0:03:55] } 10. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:22] we have the luxury of being able to do this bishop maneuver since our opponent has been wasting time in the opening and doesn't even have their king side knight developed. It's a real win if we can do this before blocking it in with Nd2.
Also, we don't want to rush with Ne4 when our opponent can still play f6 to kick us out. We would want the opponent's knight to be on f6 making the pawn push difficult to achieve. } Bd6 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 11. Be1 { [%clk 0:04:13] } Ne7 { [%clk 0:03:49] Still not threatening Ne5. An f pawn push is threatened, however. } 12. Bh4 { [%clk 0:04:08] Pinning the knight. } O-O { [%clk 0:03:46] } 13. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:04] Here Aman plays Ne5 mainly to encourage an f6 pawn push to demonstrate how to handle that. A better move might have been to develop the queenside knight. } f6 { [%clk 0:03:42] } 14. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:22] Here we have three options. 1: leave the knight there, sacking it for some other benefit. 2: If the king was still in the middle, Qh5+ 3: Move the knight back. 1 and 2 not relevant in this case so we choose the safe and simple option 3. } Ng6 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 15. Bg3 { [%clk 0:03:16] saving the bishop. It is a bad bishop but then again our opponent now has two knights on the side of the board looking a bit stupid. } Qe8 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 16. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:03:12] here e4 was tempting. One thing f6 does is it weakens e6. So anything we can do to target the e6 pawn is going to be interesting.
For now, we stick to completing our development. } e5 { [%clk 0:02:59] } 17. fxe5 { [%clk 0:03:10] Always take with the f pawn, opening up the f file to our pieces. } fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:56] } 18. e4 { [%clk 0:02:47] Notice that the e pawn is pinned to the bishop on d6.
With Re1 ideas. Looking to blast open the position given our superior piece coordination. We are ready to attack. Our opponent is not ready to defend.
As soon as Aman saw f6 he was thinking about trying to get e4 in. } dxe4 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 19. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:02:44] } Rd8 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 20. Nxd6 { [%clk 0:02:40] we will win a pawn at the end of these exchanges. } Rxd6 { [%clk 0:02:26] } 21. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Rxf1+ { [%clk 0:02:20] } 22. Rxf1 { [%clk 0:02:35] winning a pawn and developing our a1 rook. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:02:05] } 23. Bxe5 { [%clk 0:02:35] this recapture was premoved. Aman does not explain why he doesn't consider Qxe5.
Our bishop, which started off as the worst piece on the board, is now the best. } Rh6 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 24. Bd6 { [%clk 0:02:10] } Qxe2 { [%clk 0:01:54] the queen needed to stay on the back rank to guard against mate. } 25. Rf8# { [%clk 0:02:04] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

