{ https://youtu.be/6yYOQWfQwCk?si=oYT8XkdNIYK6d7N5&t=937 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:56] no threat of e5 so we can play this now. Here we discourage Ne4, Bf5 and put our bishop on a strong diagonal. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:38] threatening e5 } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:55] Stopping e5. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:53] Completing the stonewall formation, preventing Nb4. I think later Aman will prioritize Nd2 here because Ne4 is possible. } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:27] } 6. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:49] stopping Ne4 } O-O { [%clk 0:04:22] } 7. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:48] } Ne4? { [%clk 0:03:46] He can't play this } 8. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:26] } dxe4 { [%clk 0:03:44] } 9. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:26] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:03:35] } 10. O-O { [%clk 0:03:47] } (10. Bxh7+ Kxh7 11. Ng5+ Kg8 12. Qh5 Re8 13. Qh7+ Kf8 14. Qh8+ Ke7 15. Qxg7 Nd8 16. O-O { here Aman considered the Greek gift but reasoned that the attack fizzles out and so wasn't worth it. }) 10... b6 { [%clk 0:03:29] } 11. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:42] The thematic Ne5. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 12. fxe5 { [%clk 0:03:41] Always taking back with the f pawn. Gives us an e5 lance pawn. It's so strong that the second best engine move here is to sack the bishop to remove it Bxe5. } Be7?? { [%clk 0:03:14] blundering the rook. } 13. Bxa8 { [%clk 0:02:51] } (13. Rf3 Rb8 14. Bxh7+ Kxh7 15. Rh3+ Kg8 16. Qh5 f5 17. exf6 Bxf6 18. e4 { Here Aman looked at Greek gift ideas plus a rook uplift again, but again came to the conclusion that there was no check mate assuming resilient play from the opponent. }) 13... Ba6 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 14. Be4 { [%clk 0:02:23] Saving the bishop. It is better to save a full bishop than save an exchange, hence Aman not trying to move the rook. Here he pre-moves recapture of the rook by the queen Qxf1. } f5 { [%clk 0:02:46] } 15. exf6 { [%clk 0:02:18] Generally we always en passant here. } Bxf6 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 16. Rf3 { [%clk 0:02:15] Aman mouse slipped 