{ https://youtu.be/6yYOQWfQwCk?si=wD2xlFkKbE3s2oJo&t=1534 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:42] We still want to respond with e3 and capture with exd4 on cxd4 } d5 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 3. c3 { [%clk 0:04:39] solidifying the pawn structure. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:37] And now we have our stonewall pawn structure in place. Aman often sets up the pawns like this with black before developing pieces. With white it is more rare.
Preventing e5. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:21] } 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:34] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 6. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:24] here Aman changes things up a bit by prioritizing Nf3, where as previously he'd been more worried about preventing Ne4 with Nd2. He commented that one downside of Nd2 is that is blocks the DSB defense of the f pawn and could invite Bd6 from the opponent. Especially with the pawn tension between the c and d pawns this is a concern because the opponent could play Bd6... cxd4 which would force us to recapture with our c pawn opening up the c file and exposing us to queenside counterplay, while leaving our weak e pawn as a permanent weakness. } c4 { [%clk 0:03:49] We are always happy to see this move. } 7. Bc2 { [%clk 0:04:14] We always drop back to c2 like this. } Qb6 { [%clk 0:03:41] We are always happy to see this move. Our DBS is not going anywhere for a while anyway so the b2 pawn is under no real threat. } 8. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:10] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:03:28] notice this no longer scares us now c4 has been played. } 9. O-O { [%clk 0:04:07] King safety before Ne5. } Bd7 { [%clk 0:03:25] } 10. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:05] Notice that this prevents O-O-O which would immediately drop the f7 pawn to Nxf7. } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 11. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:05] We have our e5 lance pawn. Look at the kingside space we enjoy. Look how the pawn chains of both parties confine all of black's pieces to the queenside. } Ng8 { [%clk 0:02:48] A painful retreat. Engine evaluation is already +3. } 12. Qg4 { [%clk 0:03:28] Hitting g7. Aman considered Qf3 but was concerned about Nh6 in response, defending the f pawn. } g6 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 13. Qf4 { [%clk 0:02:59] targeting the f pawn. Here Aman notes that e4, while a great move to free up our minor pieces, would be bad in this case as it would allow Nxe5. (The d pawn would be pinned by the queen to the exposed king).
This move has the dual purpose, therefore, of defending the e pawn and readying e4.
Nf3 heading to Ng5 was also an option. } O-O-O { [%clk 0:02:09] drops the f pawn. } 14. e4 { [%clk 0:02:27] we're not in a rush to take the f pawn, which is not going anywhere. Better to unleash the power of the minor pieces, especially the bishops, by freeing up the diagonals.
On dxe4, Nxe4, with the knight headed for Nd6. } Rf8 { [%clk 0:02:02] } (14... dxe4 15. Nxc4 Qc7 16. Nd6+ Kb8 17. Nxf7 Rf8 18. Qxe4 Nge7 19. Nxh8 Rxh8 { a rare example of queenside play in the stonewall but resulting, like this, in a collapse on the king side. }) 15. exd5 { [%clk 0:02:07] interesting that Aman takes here, which allows exd5 recapture opening up the opponent's bishop. We have freed up our bishops, but our opponent has also freed theirs. } exd5 { [%clk 0:02:01] } 16. b3 { [%clk 0:01:55] Here Aman considered Nf3.
But here the idea is that it frees our DSB from duties of watching the b pawn. It sets up Ba3 next move attacking the rook which is defending the weak f pawn right now. } f6 { [%clk 0:01:53] } 17. Ba3 { [%clk 0:01:49] } Rf7 { [%clk 0:01:49] } 18. bxc4 { [%clk 0:01:23] } fxe5?? { [%clk 0:01:46] blunders the rook. A shame because the opponent was defending resiliently until this point. } 19. Qxf7 { [%clk 0:01:18] } Nge7 { [%clk 0:01:40] } 20. Bxe7 { [%clk 0:01:12] } exd4 { [%clk 0:01:38] } 21. c5 { [%clk 0:00:56] shutting down the diagonal to the king. } Qb5 { [%clk 0:01:29] } 22. Qf8+ { [%clk 0:00:41] } Rxf8 { [%clk 0:01:27] } 23. Rxf8+ { [%clk 0:00:35] } Kc7 { [%clk 0:01:26] } 24. Bd6# { [%clk 0:00:26] savage. } 1-0

