 looks like we end in a draw with best play in the case the opponent doesn't capture the knight and instead tries to boot it with f6 }) 12. fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:59] taking back with the pawn and not the knight! The goal is to establish an e5 lance pawn which will spearhead our attacks on the king. } Qb6 { [%clk 0:02:29] This is actually a move. The threat is NOT b2. It is that the queen pins the d pawn which makes Nxe5 a threat. } 13. Qe1 { [%clk 0:02:04] defending the e pawn. Kh1 was also an option. } (13. Bd2 Nxe5 14. Nxe5 Bxe5 15. Qf3 Bf6 { losing a pawn. }) 13... Bg4 { [%clk 0:02:16] renewing the threat of Nxe5 after Bxf3, Rxf3.
Note we can't move our bishop or we'll drop the b2 pawn. } 14. Kh1 { [%clk 0:01:41] breaking the pin on the d4 pawn. } e6 { [%clk 0:02:06] } 15. Qh4 { [%clk 0:01:29] Our queen wants to be on this square. Hitting the bishop and adding pressure in front of the king. Bh6 and Ng5 follow up ideas. } Bxf3!? { [%clk 0:02:01] Probably a good move given the circumstances, although the computer hates it. } 16. Rxf3 { [%clk 0:01:24] Rook uplift. Rh3 and Bh6 coming in as follows. } Ne7 { [%clk 0:01:47] } 17. Rh3 { [%clk 0:00:47] On h5, g4 to crack open the kingside pawns.
Playing g4 first is also an option and according to the engine is actually a better move. } (17. Bh6 Nf5 18. Bxf5 exf5 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 20. g4 { Aman was not thrilled with this line, trading off his LSB. }) 17... h5 { [%clk 0:01:45] } 18. g4 { [%clk 0:00:45] } Qc7 { [%clk 0:00:31] } 19. Bg5 { [%clk 0:00:36] no longer drops the b pawn. Aman may have played it anyway. Bringing another piece into the attack. Not only the bishop but also the a1 rook. } Kf8 { [%clk 0:00:20] } 20. Rf1 { [%clk 0:00:32] } Rac8 { [%clk 0:00:14] } (20... hxg4 21. Qh8+ Bxh8 22. Rxh8+ Ng8 23. Bh6+ Ke7 24. Rxf7+ Kxf7 (24... Kd8 25. Rxc7 Kxc7 26. Bxg6 Re7 { position is pretty equal as long as the king doesn't fall for the rook capture. }) 25. Rh7# { Here Aman pointed out an interesting (and rare) mate that could have occurred is this one where 4 of black's pieces contribute to the king having no squares. However, if the king had refused the rook capture and gone to d8 black escapes only slightly worse. }) 21. Rhf3 { [%clk 0:00:19] we can move the rook now because a rook queen battery is no longer needed on the h file. Also, by leaving the g4 pawn there (by not capturing gxh5) we keep the knight out of f5. } Nf5 { [%clk 0:00:05] sacking the knight to delay the inevitable. Best engine move even though it really doesn't do much. } (21... a6 22. Rxf7+ Kg8 23. Bxe7 Qxe7 24. Rxe7 Rf8 25. Qg5 Rxf1+ 26. Bxf1 Kf8 27. Rd7 Re8 28. Qxg6 Re7 29. Rd8+ Re8 30. Qxe8#) 22. gxf5 { [%clk 0:00:17] } exf5 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 23. Bxf5 { [%clk 0:00:14] } gxf5 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 24. Rxf5 { [%clk 0:00:12] 1-0 White wins on time. } 1-0

