[Event "Opponent leaves knight on e5, Greek gift — Urano8 (760)"]
[Site "Chess.com"]
[Date "2023.03.18"]
[Round "-"]
[White "wonestall"]
[Black "Urano8"]
[Result "1-0"]
[TimeControl "300"]
[ECO "A80"]
[WhiteElo "746"]
[BlackElo "760"]
[PlyCount "43"]

{ https://youtu.be/N2T3qPMCtBU?si=08m2nN9CcizgQf_B&t=1700 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:58] The Dutch. Opponent may set up a stonewall themselves. } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:53] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:49] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:46] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:44] completing our pawn set up. } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:41] Poor move, blocking the d pawn. } 6. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:26] Not strictly necessary, but making sure to stop Ne4 ahead of time, even though we could deal with it later if we wanted. } (6. Nf3 Ne4 7. Bxe4 fxe4 8. Ng5 Be7 9. Nxe4 { our opponent cannot defend the pawn }) 6... O-O { [%clk 0:04:39] } 7. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:22] } Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 8. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:18] "one move attack, one move defense". } Re8 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 9. O-O { [%clk 0:04:16] } a6 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 10. h3 { [%clk 0:04:12] boot the knight before playing Ne5.
Getting our "snork" escape square with tempo. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:05] } 11. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:11] } b5 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 12. e4 { [%clk 0:03:42] Often if our opponent doesn't capture our e5 knight this is a plan B. This is how we will free up our d2 knight and DSB.
If we can push e4 it usually means we can combine other opening / attacking ideas with the stonewall and get the best of both worlds. Usually the weakness of the stonewall is the backwards e pawn so to be able to push it like this is generally going to work in our favor. } b4 { [%clk 0:03:19] } 13. exf5 { [%clk 0:03:31] Aman plans Bxf5 recapture, simply winning a pawn.
Another option on exf5 would be Bc4+, with the king not having many safe squares to escape to. } (13. Nxc6 dxc6 14. e5 a5 15. exf6 gxf6 { In the post game analysis, Aman explained that while this sequence was playable and winning a piece, he wanted to demonstrate the main idea of leaving the knight on e5 in the stonewall, and pointed out that it was this knight that ultimately made the greek gift checkmate work later in the game. }) 13... bxc3 { [%clk 0:03:12] } 14. bxc3 { [%clk 0:03:25] } Bb7 { [%clk 0:03:04] } 15. Ndf3 { [%clk 0:03:01] we can play this now as we are no longer worried about Ne4. } Nd5 { [%clk 0:03:02] "good move" although Stockfish doesn't agree. } 16. Bd2 { [%clk 0:02:53] Developing the DSB, guarding the c and f pawns. } Rb8 { [%clk 0:02:47] } 17. fxe6 { [%clk 0:02:45] "now all our pieces are in place I will take here". With Greek Gift ideas. } dxe6 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 18. Bxh7+ { [%clk 0:02:22] The reason this is playable is not that we have the knight ready to jump to g5 and the queen to h5 because a resource is Nf6 and the h7 square is covered. The reason is that we have Qf7 available. } Kxh7 { [%clk 0:02:34] } 19. Ng5+ { [%clk 0:02:20] } Kg8 { [%clk 0:02:27] } 20. Qh5 { [%clk 0:02:18] } Kf8 { [%clk 0:02:07] } (20... Nf6 21. Qf7+ Kh8 22. Ng6# { this is what makes the greek gift work, even if our opponent had have found Nf6. }) 21. Ng6+ { [%clk 0:02:08] when you have mate in 1, look for better. } Kg8 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 22. Qh7# { [%clk 0:02:06] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

