{ https://youtu.be/N2T3qPMCtBU?si=HnEW5vh_oV3rdTPj&t=3061 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:54] we immediately challenge for the diagonal. Premove recapture with the queen. } Bxd3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4. Qxd3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 5. f4 { [%clk 0:04:50] preventing e5 } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 6. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:45] Preventing Ne4. } Nb4 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 7. Qb5+ { [%clk 0:04:37] Attacking the knight with a check. Forces the knight to go back. } (7. Qe2 Nxc2+ { allowing a fork if we just simply retreat. }) 7... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 8. c3 { [%clk 0:04:34] we could take b7 here but remember that our stonewall is all about the kingside. We don't want to play on the queenside if we can avoid it. We want the queenside locked up, if anything, with the pawns in place. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:15] } 9. Qd3 { [%clk 0:04:30] Going back to prevent Ne4 again. Note the knight can not come back to attack our queen thanks to the c pawn push. } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 10. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:29] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:10] } 11. O-O { [%clk 0:04:28] } a6 { [%clk 0:04:08] } 12. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:26] } Nxe5?? { [%clk 0:04:01] blundering the pawn fork. } 13. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:26] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:00] the better move than saving the bishop. Gains a pawn as compensation and can be followed up by Nd7 hitting the weak e pawn. } 14. dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:13] } Ne4? { [%clk 0:02:24] blundering the pawn. } (14... Nd7 15. Nf3 { defending the pawn. }) 15. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:10] } dxe4 { [%clk 0:02:23] } 16. Qxe4 { [%clk 0:04:10] } f5 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 17. exf6 { [%clk 0:04:08] en passant practically forced in the stonewall. } Rxf6 { [%clk 0:02:17] } 18. Rxf6 { [%clk 0:04:04] trading off into a winning end game. } Qxf6 { [%clk 0:02:14] } 19. Qf3 { [%clk 0:03:58] } (19. Qxb7 Rf8 20. Qxc7 Qf1# { an example of how greedy pawn hunting can go wrong. If you are already up a piece you don't need more material to win! }) 19... Qg5 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 20. e4 { [%clk 0:03:56] freeing up the bishop. } Qc5+ { [%clk 0:01:55] } 21. Be3 { [%clk 0:03:50] developing with tempo. } Qd6 { [%clk 0:01:49] } 22. Rf1 { [%clk 0:03:08] } Rf8 { [%clk 0:01:44] friendly } 23. Qxf8+ { [%clk 0:03:05] don't have to ask us twice. } Qxf8 { [%clk 0:01:42] } 24. Rxf8+ { [%clk 0:03:05] } Kxf8 { [%clk 0:01:42] and now, all that is needed is to convert with the extra piece. } 25. Kf2 { [%clk 0:03:03] prioritizing activation of the king. } Kf7 { [%clk 0:01:40] } 26. Bf4 { [%clk 0:03:01] } Kg6 { [%clk 0:01:40] } 27. Bxc7 { [%clk 0:03:00] just like that we have a queenside majority. } Kg5 { [%clk 0:01:39] } 28. Be5 { [%clk 0:02:59] the opponent's king is in a mini prison here. } g6 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 29. Kf3 { [%clk 0:02:52] } b5 { [%clk 0:01:27] } 30. Bg7 { [%clk 0:02:38] Aman doesn't explain his strategy here but presumably he's taking away escape squares from the king to potentially set up a mate with bishop or pawn. } Kh4 { [%clk 0:01:19] } 31. b3 { [%clk 0:02:10] A waiting move. Aman is plotting how to mate with the bishop. Needs the opponent to move their h pawn. } h5 32. c4 bxc4 33. bxc4 g5 34. c5 g4+ 35. Kf4 g3 36. Bf6# { game could have finished like this. At first I thought containment of the king was to put him in jail to make getting a passed pawn easier. But that would not be a smart strategy because of the risk of stalemate. Instead, the creation of the passed pawn would just be like waiting moves to force opponent to move their pawns and eventually set up a mate like this. } 1-0

