{ https://youtu.be/Uri85gMC5KI?si=Qk20qBpgVZYjxwbX&t=465 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:55] threatening e5 } 3. f4 { [%clk 0:04:54] stopping e5 } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 4. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:49] keeping the knight out of b4. } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 6. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:43] keeping the knight out of e4 } O-O { [%clk 0:04:38] } 7. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:14] } Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:16] threatening the weak e pawn } 8. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:03] guarding the weak e pawn. } Re8 { [%clk 0:04:09] } 9. Ne5 { [%clk 0:02:41] interesting case of playing the Ne5 jump before castling. } (9. Bxh7+ { In the post game analysis, chess com gives this as a brilliancy. But the position we are left with after the dust settles, despite winning a pawn, is very hard to play. Aman discusses how we have to be very careful in the stonewall when giving up our LSB. It is one of our strongest pieces, and without it, we are very weak on the light squares, especially if our opponent still has their LSB. } Kxh7 10. Ng5+ Kg8 11. Qxg4 f6 12. Qh5 { We need to sac another piece like this in order to stay better. } (12. Ngf3 e5 { The more natural knight retreat leaves us worse: -1.8 according to the engine. }) 12... fxg5 13. fxg5 Ne7 14. g6 Nxg6 15. Qxg6 Rf8 16. Nf3 Rf6 17. Qc2 Qe8 18. e4 dxe4 19. Qxe4 Bd7 { Aman doesn't like this line as the opponent gets too much counterplay with f6. We are left with a knight and a bad bishop against the bishop pair. }) (9. h3 Nh6 10. O-O Ne7 11. Ne5 { Another option is to boot the knight before we play Ne5. But the whole point of Ne5 is to have our opponent take us, so we can establish that e5 lance pawn. So, the game continuation also forces resolution of the knight situation but in a way that is more likely to lead to us getting that main objective of establishing an e5 lance pawn. }) 9... Ncxe5?? { [%clk 0:04:01] dropping a piece. } 10. fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:40] both bishop and knight are under attack. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:52] taking a pawn on the way out. } 11. dxe5 { [%clk 0:02:36] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:51] two pawns for the piece. Central pawns too. The engine give this as +2.7, though. } 12. Bxh7+ { [%clk 0:02:23] Now with the Greek gift idea. } (12. Qh5 { this forks mate and the bishop, but our opponent can respond with f5 to guard both. } f5) 12... Kxh7 { [%clk 0:03:47] } 13. Qh5+ { [%clk 0:02:20] Forking bishop and check. } Kg8 { [%clk 0:03:41] } 14. Qxe5 { [%clk 0:02:20] } f6 { [%clk 0:03:38] our opponent actually has half decent compensation with these strong central pawns even though a piece down. } 15. Qg3 { [%clk 0:02:14] } e5 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 16. O-O { [%clk 0:02:10] } Kf7?! { [%clk 0:03:28] looking to bring the rook to the semi open h file. } 17. e4 { [%clk 0:01:58] Our e4 break. Opening up the DSB. } Rh8 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 18. Nf3 { [%clk 0:01:50] } (18. Qxe5 Be6 19. Qf4 dxe4 20. Nxe4 Bd5 { There was this line taking advantage of the pin available but Aman didn't play because he considered it greedy pawn grabbing and elected to prioritize using his extra piece. If we don't bring all of our pieces into the game we can be up material in theory but not benefit from it in practice. }) 18... Ke7 { [%clk 0:02:53] } (18... dxe4 19. Nxe5+ Kg8 20. Ng6 { On takes this continuation was planned. }) 19. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:01:44] } fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 20. Qxe5+ { [%clk 0:01:20] } (20. Bg5+ Kd6 21. Bxd8 { Could have won the queen immediately but there was chances for mate with the game continuation Aman chose. }) 20... Be6 { [%clk 0:02:47] } (20... Kd7 21. Qxd5+ Ke7 22. Bg5+ Ke8 23. Qxd8# { If our opponent had played Kd7 it would have led to mate. }) 21. Bg5+ { [%clk 0:01:17] winning the queen. } Kd7 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 22. Bxd8 { [%clk 0:01:14] This is a little better than if we'd just played Bg5+ right away. We have an extra pawn and a slightly better position. } Raxd8 { [%clk 0:02:41] } 23. exd5 { [%clk 0:01:11] } Bg4 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 24. Rf7+ { [%clk 0:01:09] } Kc8 { [%clk 0:02:24] } 25. Qxc7# { [%clk 0:01:09] Aman premoves this checkmate. } 1-0

