{ https://youtu.be/Uri85gMC5KI?si=X4puSlZ_sGOp-7KZ&t=1507 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:59] threatening e5 } 3. f4 { [%clk 0:04:55] preventing e5 } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 4. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:54] stopping Bf5 } Qd6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:50] preventing Nb4 } Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 6. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:48] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 7. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:44] Preventing Ne4 } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:42] } Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 9. Qc2 { [%clk 0:03:46] defending the bishop. Better than Qe2 because it also stops Ne4. } (9. Qe2 Ne4 { would have allowed Ne4. }) (9. Bxf5 exf5 10. Ne5 O-O 11. h3 Nd7 12. Qf3 f6 { if we trade bishops like this, we end up with the worst of both worlds--we allow the e4 square to our opponent while allowing him to deny us e5 like this. }) 9... Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:29] Qc2 also had the advantage of unpinning our knight so this move does not accomplish anything for our opponent. } 10. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:43] achieving our thematic jump. } Ne4?? { [%clk 0:04:21] can't do that, pal. } 11. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:03:33] interestingly Stockfish wanted us to capture initially with the bishop but our rule of thumb is to always capture with the knight first unless we have good reason not to. } (11. Bxe4 dxe4 12. Nxg4 f5 13. Nc4 Qd5 { stockfish seems to prefer to capture with bishop then recapture with knight because it allows us to attack the queen like this? Winning tempi? }) (11. Nxg4 f5 12. Nf2 g5 { we could've taken the free piece here rather than win the pawn with the knight exchange but Aman explains that the positional advantage we enjoy now makes it far more valuable to have taken the e4 knight rather than chase after the hanging bishop. Winning the bishop after the fact is just a bonus on top of our huge positional advantage. This is not so much about engine evaluation as it is about having a comfortable, playable position with a ton of attacking options and few defensive weaknesses. }) 11... dxe4 { [%clk 0:04:20] } 12. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:03:33] } Na5?? { [%clk 0:04:18] knights on the rim are dim. And in this case also dropping a piece. } 13. Nxg4 { [%clk 0:03:26] if it's free it's for me. } Bh4 { [%clk 0:04:16] a friendly move, threatening nothing. } (13... f5 { our opponent missed this pawn fork. }) 14. Qa4+ { [%clk 0:02:10] Aman breaking his own rules here and switching to queenside play. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 15. Ne5 { [%clk 0:02:06] knights to the middle. } O-O { [%clk 0:03:50] } 16. g3 { [%clk 0:01:49] } Bf6 { [%clk 0:03:48] } (16... Nxe5 17. fxe5 { if he would have taken here it would have led to him losing a piece as the queen is attacked by the knight capture. Hence throwing in g3 before we look to trade the knight with Nxc6 as a potential follow up next move. }) 17. Qc2 { [%clk 0:01:30] Aman suspects our opponent is going to take Nxe5 which will result in a pawn fork of his queen and bishop upon the fxe5 recapture, hence this waiting move. It's still a useful move as it creates a queen, bishop battery attacking the opponent's h pawn. We are giving up a free queenside pawn, however. (although we don't care about the queenside, and indeed, "free pawns" can lead to open lines and queenside counterpa } (17. Nxc6 bxc6 18. Qxc6 Qxc6 19. Bxc6 { simplifying and winning a pawn. }) 17... Nxe5?? { [%clk 0:03:44] blundering the fork. Although our opponent's position is so bad this is actually one of the top engine moves. } 18. fxe5 { [%clk 0:01:28] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:42] } 19. dxe5 { [%clk 0:01:26] } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:03:32] } 20. Bxh7+ { [%clk 0:01:22] And here the battery proves useful. } Kh8 { [%clk 0:03:31] } 21. Bd3 { [%clk 0:01:20] picking his pocket and getting the hell out of dodge. } g6 { [%clk 0:03:29] } 22. c4 { [%clk 0:01:12] anticipating Kg7, putting the king on the same diagonal as the queen, we play c3 to open up the diagonal. (pawn break to make room for a piece) } Kg7 { [%clk 0:03:25] } 23. Bd2 { [%clk 0:01:09] looks like an innocent developing move, connecting the rooks, but has some nasty intent our opponent may well miss. } Qh5! { [%clk 0:03:20] credit to our opponent for seeing the danger. } 24. Bc3+ { [%clk 0:01:05] Bc3+ is still a strong move. } f6 { [%clk 0:03:14] } 25. Rf4 { [%clk 0:00:58] Rh4 ideas. Also, on Rh8, Bxf6+ and skewering the rook. } Rh8 { [%clk 0:03:09] } 26. Bxf6+ { [%clk 0:00:54] } Kf7 { [%clk 0:03:05] } 27. Bxg6+ { [%clk 0:00:50] nasty. } Qxg6 { [%clk 0:02:58] } 28. Bxh8+ { [%clk 0:00:47] nasty. } Qf6 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 29. Qh7+ { [%clk 0:00:41] } Ke8 { [%clk 0:02:49] } 30. Bxf6 { [%clk 0:00:38] ruthless } e5 { [%clk 0:02:45] } 31. Qe7# { [%clk 0:00:36] mercy kill. } 1-0

