{ https://youtu.be/Uri85gMC5KI?si=zl3QrzK9Icjbx9tZ&t=2045 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:55] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:53] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:51] not a big deal, but nice to get this diagonal before our opponent can. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:52] threatening e5 } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:49] stopping e5 } e6 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:47] preventing Nb4. Interesting this over Nd2. } (5. Nd2 Nb4 6. Be2 c5 7. c3 Nc6 8. Bd3 { we can play this way and be fine even if we initially allow Nb4 }) 5... Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 6. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:40] preventing Ne4 } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:15] } 7. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:36] Preparing to O-O, and with Ne5 ideas. } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:30] king safety before Ne5 } Ne4?? { [%clk 0:03:58] can't do that, bud. } 9. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:25] } dxe4 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 10. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:25] } f5! { [%clk 0:03:53] } 11. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:23] } h6 { [%clk 0:03:49] } 12. e4 { [%clk 0:04:08] if we can play e4, we usually want to. getting rid of our weakness. Opening up the diagonal for our DSB. (I believe that Ne5 followed by recapture of the knight by the f pawn, to create an e5 "lance pawn" is not as effective as when our opponent has already played f4. An f pawn push is often desirable for the opponent playing the stonewall, to free their position, but the e5 pawn prevents this. If they can get in f4 before we can establish the e5 lance pawn it is often a positional win for them.) } g5?! { [%clk 0:03:37] } (12... O-O 13. b4) (12... fxe4 13. Bxe4 { the bishop is dominating two diagonals. }) 13. exf5 { [%clk 0:03:54] we start by taking here with the aim of opening up the diagonal for the bishop. } exf5 { [%clk 0:03:31] } 14. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:53] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:29] } 15. fxe5 { [%clk 0:03:52] achieving our e5 lance pawn. } f4 { [%clk 0:03:21] trying to save the pawn. But look at how exposed that king is. } 16. Qh5+ { [%clk 0:03:44] } Kf8 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 17. Bxf4!! { [%clk 0:03:42] The position is so strong we can just sac everything to open lines and bring our forces to bear on the king. } (17. Rxf4+ { Rxf4 was actually stronger. } gxf4 18. Bxf4 Qe8 19. Bxh6+ Kg8 20. Bc4+ Kh7 21. Bf7 Qxf7 22. Qxf7+ Kxh6 23. Qxe7 Raf8 24. Qxd7 Rh7 25. Qg4) 17... Be6 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 18. Bxg5+ { [%clk 0:03:27] discovering check. } Kg7 { [%clk 0:02:28] } 19. Qg6# { [%clk 0:03:25] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

