{ https://youtu.be/Uri85gMC5KI?si=_C9LaKgHkHf8xwPQ&t=3023 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } b6 { [%clk 0:04:54] The bishop will come to b7. If we don't get our knight out fast it will cause us problems on the a8-h1 diagonal. } 3. f4 { [%clk 0:04:46] } (3. Bd3 Bb7 4. f4 Bxg2 { oops. }) 3... Bb7 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:45] solving all the problems on the diagonal, for now. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:42] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:47] now Ne4 is threatened. } 6. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:38] stopping Ne4 } e6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 7. c3 { [%clk 0:04:37] Stopping Nb4 } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:33] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:42] } 9. Qc2 { [%clk 0:02:29] There is an important difference in this position that we have not seen so-far in our speed run--the bishop covering the e4 square through the knight and pawn. Qc2 is necessary to add another defender to this square if we want to prevent Ne4.
Other options were Qe1 or Qe2 with the idea of supporting an e pawn push. Aman (correctly, according to the engine) suggests that this e pawn push idea supported by the queen on e1 or e2 is probably the objectively stronger move. He still likes Qc2, but the issue with it is that it leaves our queen somewhat misplaced on the queenside a few moves down the line when we are going to wish it was on the king side to join the attack.
Still, Aman goes for this Qc2 because he wants to showcase it and perhaps because it is closer to the spirit of the opening as he is trying to teach us to play it. } (9. Ne5 Nxe5 10. fxe5 Ne4 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Bxe4 Bxe4 { This is why we needed Qc2 before playing our Ne5 jump. }) 9... Na5 { [%clk 0:04:25] doesn't really threaten anything. ignore. } 10. Ne5 { [%clk 0:02:22] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:04:22] can't do that, bud. } 11. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:02:18] } dxe4 { [%clk 0:04:21] } 12. Bxe4 { [%clk 0:02:18] } Bxe4 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 13. Qxe4 { [%clk 0:02:18] Aman's instincts were right. } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 14. Bd2 { [%clk 0:02:07] we are not concerned about Bxe5 obviously so we take the time to develop our DSB and connect our rooks. } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 15. fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:07] } Nc4 { [%clk 0:04:06] } 16. Be1 { [%clk 0:02:00] continuing our DSB maneuver. The engine actually hates this move and wanted us to put the DSB back(!). Aman is sacking a pawn here. (although the engine does not want black to take it). } Nxb2 { [%clk 0:03:59] } (16... Qd5 17. Qxd5 exd5 { The engine thinks this is close to even / slightly winning for white. }) 17. Bh4 { [%clk 0:01:55] completing our maneuver. } Qd7 { [%clk 0:03:51] } 18. Rf3 { [%clk 0:01:22] with ideas to double the rooks and also play Rg3 or Rh3. } (18. Bf6 gxf6 19. exf6 (19. Qh4 Qd5 20. Rf3 Nd3 21. Rg3+ Kh8 22. Qxf6# { Aman missed this attack whereby we don't take back with the f pawn but instead delay the recapture and take with the queen leading to mate. }) 19... Kh8 { Aman thought this line "not quite winning" but in fact it is winning for black according to the engine. But he is correct in his analysis that the piece sacrifice does not break through, *if* we take back with the f pawn. }) 18... Nc4 { [%clk 0:03:42] threatening to fork the queen and rook with Nd2. But our attack is so strong we can give up the exchange. } 19. Bf6 { [%clk 0:00:48] } Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:38] } (19... gxf6 20. exf6 Kh8 21. Rh3 h6 22. Rxh6+ Kg8 23. Qg4# { Aman was planning to mate like this on gxf6 }) 20. Qg4 { [%clk 0:00:37] } Nxf3+ { [%clk 0:03:33] } 21. gxf3 { [%clk 0:00:32] } g6 { [%clk 0:03:28] } 22. Qg5 { [%clk 0:00:27] } Rfd8 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 23. Qh6 { [%clk 0:00:10] preventing the king's escape.
Aman comments here that this is the ideal mating net with bishop and queen. Bishop on f6 and queen on h6. The bishop really "digs into" the position on f6, hitting the rook etc. And the queen can also be deployed in other mating patterns on the h file such as a rook uplift there and mating on h8, for example. } Qe7 { [%clk 0:02:52] } 24. Qg7# { [%clk 0:00:05] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

