{ https://youtu.be/LrJH6AeNLCk?si=xqyxfzrixvLa5LeC&t=1927 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. f4 { [%clk 0:04:53] technically e5 is possible so we start out here with f4. } b6 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:04:37] } Bb7 { [%clk 0:04:48] threatening Bxg2 winning our rook. Whenever we see b6 we should be thinking about this and aiming to get our knight to f3 to prevent it. } 4. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:34] } h6 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 5. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:31] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:29] Castling. Our knight is well controlled here, so we are not going to be able to get in Ne5 just get (if at all). } g6 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 7. f5 { [%clk 0:03:10] Aman explains here that if the e pawn was on e6 here, he would continue with our more regular plans--c3... Nd2... and maybe an e4 push. But given the opponent isn't castled and is set up very passively here Aman elects for a more direct and aggressive option in an attempt to open things up to open up lines of attack to the king. } g5 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 8. e4 { [%clk 0:03:01] } f6 { [%clk 0:04:23] } 9. Ba6 { [%clk 0:02:08] Not a mouse slip. Here we get Dark Magician Hambo giving a masterclass in playing the man vs playing the board. If we could only get the queen to h5 it would actually be checkmate here. The Dark Magician rejects moves such as Ne5 (the opponent may take Nxe5 facilitating, deliberately or otherwise, a backwards knight move to block the check), or Ne4 as "too obvious". } Bxa6 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 10. Nfd2 { [%clk 0:01:58] "defending the rook" 🤣 } Bxf1 { [%clk 0:04:09] } 11. Qh5# { [%clk 0:01:55] The Dark Magician strikes again. } 1-0

