{ https://youtu.be/Rt93_cWZUUA?si=XmEQSoey57Hx3HkC&t=13 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } g6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:50] In a previous game (and in a later game) Aman plays c3 here. He does not explain why f4 is chosen here over c3. He does mention that he wants to get the knight out to f3 ASAP. } e6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:53] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:46] } a6 { [%clk 0:04:51] Here Aman mentions that if d5 we will play Nd2 to prevent Ne4. (However, it seems more subtle than that because he plays Nd2 anyway in some games and this seems due to the threat of e5 and that we can't stop e5 but that having a knight on d2 covers e4 which prevents a follow up e4 push, forking our pieces in many lines, supported by the knight on f6, thus giving us more flexibility in response to e4 (i.e. we have the option to ignore it).
He maybe b5... Bb7 planned by the opponent? } 7. c3 { [%clk 0:04:23] Completing our pawn formation and "seeing what he wants to do". } b5 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 8. Bd2 { [%clk 0:03:53] looks like Bb7 is coming threatening e4. But Aman elects to go for the DSB maneuver here given that it will be strong (coming with a pin) if we can land Bh4. We would always have Qc2 if we wanted to add support to e4 } Bb7 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 9. Be1 { [%clk 0:03:51] } d6 { [%clk 0:04:42] } 10. Bh4 { [%clk 0:03:48] } Qe8 { [%clk 0:04:33] breaking the pin. } 11. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:03:45] } Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 12. Qe2 { [%clk 0:03:39] defending e3 } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 13. h3 { [%clk 0:02:54] here e4 was also playable but Aman elects for this. } Nh6 { [%clk 0:04:21] } 14. e4 { [%clk 0:02:49] e4 push. Now makes even more sense as it stops Nf5 } f5 { [%clk 0:04:17] } 15. e5 { [%clk 0:02:15] } (15. exf5 Nxf5 { taking here would be a mistake as it would let the knight back into the game. }) (15. Rae1 fxe4 16. Bxe4 { This was also playable. }) 15... dxe5 { [%clk 0:04:15] } 16. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:02:10] by hook or by crook we get our Ne5. My instinct in games is often to recapture with the pawn here, leaving the knight bad. } (16. fxe5 { An inaccuracy. }) 16... Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 17. fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:04] } Qc6 { [%clk 0:03:45] Not actually threatening anything right now. } 18. Nf3 { [%clk 0:01:33] } f4 { [%clk 0:03:41] } 19. Bg5 { [%clk 0:01:26] attacking the f4 pawn. } Nf5 { [%clk 0:03:32] } 20. Bxf4 { [%clk 0:01:20] } Nxd4?? { [%clk 0:03:25] } 21. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:01:11] discovers defense of the bishop and hits the queen. Queen is also guarding the pawn (so bishop, queen battery is not a threat). } Rxf4?? { [%clk 0:03:19] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

