{ https://youtu.be/p0x1ktjbDO4?si=u-uTsUU9dJgPA2I3&t=3261 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } a6 { [%clk 0:04:57] what is this move? } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:53] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:52] "Now that he's threatening Bg4 we want to be able to play Nf3". Not sure why not Nd2 here. Seems like something to do with the LSB not being blocked in. We can see Aman played a similar move here: https://lichess.org/study/jjmB1AcS/k2AzWYqE } (4. Nd2 Bg4 5. f3 Bh5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Qb3 e5 { It seems this is the problem. }) 4... Bg4 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 6. c3 { [%clk 0:04:49] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 7. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:46] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:41] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:34] } 9. Qc2 { [%clk 0:04:27] Aman explains that Qe1 was also a good move here. Idea is to break the pin to prepare Ne5. He chooses the play Qc2 here because he's already demonstrated Qe1 a few times. We often see this Qc2 idea when we want to develop Nd2 but need to keep watch over the LSB before it is playable. } (9. Qe1 Bf5 10. Qe2 Bxd3 11. Qxd3) 9... h6 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 10. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:25] } Bf5 { [%clk 0:04:14] } 11. Bxf5 { [%clk 0:04:21] } exf5 { [%clk 0:04:13] } 12. Qxf5 { [%clk 0:04:20] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:10] } 13. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:20] } Nh7 { [%clk 0:03:56] } 14. e4 { [%clk 0:04:18] } g6 { [%clk 0:03:48] } 15. Qh3 { [%clk 0:04:12] } h5 { [%clk 0:03:41] } 16. exd5 { [%clk 0:03:56] } Qxd5 { [%clk 0:03:35] } 17. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:55] } c5 { [%clk 0:03:21] } 18. Bh6 { [%clk 0:03:51] } Rfe8 { [%clk 0:03:11] } 19. Rf2 { [%clk 0:03:33] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:03:09] } 20. cxd4 { [%clk 0:03:31] } Qc6 { [%clk 0:02:46] } 21. Raf1 { [%clk 0:03:24] } f6 { [%clk 0:02:20] } 22. Qg3 { [%clk 0:03:07] } fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:15] } 23. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:04] } Qe6 { [%clk 0:02:07] } 24. Nxg6 { [%clk 0:02:57] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:01:41] } 25. Nxe7+ { [%clk 0:02:49] } Kh7 { [%clk 0:01:30] } 26. Qg7# { [%clk 0:02:49] 1-