{ https://youtu.be/yUFSsm7s3-E?si=vbqIAR27ok1HdxF0&t=11 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 4. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:51] Aman plays an early Nd2 around 9 times against this set up. He doesn't explain why here. In an earlier game (vs radekmiszewski) he said he did it "because I just don't want to deal with Ne4". I think it has to do with this opening being slow/ passive by our opponent so it gives us the luxury to play this Nd2 move nice and early and completely shut down ideas of Ne4 from our opponent. } c5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:49] } c4 { [%clk 0:04:57] Not a good sign from big D } 6. Bc2 { [%clk 0:04:47] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 7. f4 { [%clk 0:04:46] } b6 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 8. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:44] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 9. O-O { [%clk 0:04:42] } Bb7 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 10. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:41] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 11. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:41] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 12. exd6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:39] } 13. e4 { [%clk 0:04:32] pawn push to crack open the center, activate our knight/DSB. } dxe4 { [%clk 0:04:32] } 14. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:30] } f5 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 15. Ng5 { [%clk 0:04:27] Qh5 ideas to follow } Qf6 { [%clk 0:04:17] } 16. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:11] Nf3... Ne5 reroute ideas. } h6 { [%clk 0:04:14] } 17. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:10] } Qg6 { [%clk 0:04:00] } 18. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:05] Rae1 ideas to follow } Rae8 { [%clk 0:03:53] } 19. Rae1 { [%clk 0:04:03] } e5 { [%clk 0:03:38] } 20. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:40] looking for trades. Not concerned about the pawn on d6. } (20. dxe5 { this is a "great move" according to Aman, but he decides to go for trades, being up material and all. }) 20... Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 21. Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:37] } Re6 { [%clk 0:03:31] } 22. Qf2 { [%clk 0:03:28] targeting the weak pawn. (but with Re3... Rg3 ideas) } (22. Qxc4 Qxg2# { A good way to get mated. }) (22. Bxf5 Rxf5 23. Rxf5 Qxf5 24. d7 Qg5 25. Bf6 Rxe2 26. d8=Q+ { even sacking bishop, rook, and queen is winning. } Re8 27. Qxe8+ Kh7 28. Bxg5 Bxg2 29. Kxg2 a6 30. Re7 a5 31. Qf7 a4 32. Qxg7#) 22... Rfe8 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 23. Bxf5 { [%clk 0:03:26] And Big D throws in the towel. } 1-0

