{ https://youtu.be/NJQGPORLNA0?si=GxsXNTrqFM-poNv5&t=3457 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. c3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 4. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 5. f4 { [%clk 0:04:49] Interesting Aman plays f4 rather than Nd2 here. He doesn't mention why. Possibly an early f4 here is played because Nd2 would block defense of the f pawn from the DSB such that our opponent could play cxd4 and force us to recapture with cxd4 } (5. Nd2 Nc6 6. f4 Bd6 7. Ngf3 cxd4 8. cxd4 { Possibly he held back on Nd2 for this reason? }) 5... Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 6. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:33] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:45] } (6... Bd6 7. Ngf3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Nb4 9. Bb1 { Later on in the video Aman raises his concern after playing Nd2 that this kind of line is possible, where our opponent plays Bd6, puts pressure on our f pawn, and after cxd4 we are forced to recapture cxd4 allowing the knight to enter our position and annoy us. }) 7. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:32] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:42] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:30] } h6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 9. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:28] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:39] } 10. fxe5 { [%clk 0:04:28] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 11. e4? { [%clk 0:03:37] Aman plays e4 here which the engine evaluates as an inaccuracy/mistake. Aman said it himself, but this allows cxd4. } (11. Qh5 { The engine wanted this queen move. } c4 12. Bc2 f5 13. g4 fxg4 14. Qxg4 Rxf1+ 15. Nxf1 Qf8 16. Qxe6+ Qf7 17. Qxf7+ Kxf7 18. e4 { we often want to time e4 for after our opponent has played c4. }) 11... Nb6 { [%clk 0:04:20] } (11... cxd4 12. cxd4 Qb6 { This is the line our opponent should have played. This is slightly better for black, according to the engine. }) 12. Qg4 { [%clk 0:03:22] } (12. Nf3 { This is also a good move, but it blocks the queen coming out. So, Aman chose to move the queen out first. }) 12... dxe4 { [%clk 0:03:42] } 13. Qxe4? { [%clk 0:03:20] Sets up a check mate threat with a battery. } (13. Nxe4 { engine wants this recapture. } f5 14. exf6 Rxf6 15. Nxf6+ Bxf6 16. Bxh6 Qf8 17. Rxf6 Qxf6 18. Bg5 Nd7 19. Rf1 Kf7 20. Bxf6 gxf6 21. Qg6+ Ke7 22. dxc5 e5 23. Qg7+ Kd8 24. Rxf6 b6 25. Rc6 e4 26. Qg8+ Nf8 27. Qxf8+ Kd7 28. Bxe4 a6 29. Qd6+ Ke8 30. Bg6# { Yes, we could have been forked, but we could take en passant and be totally winning, like this. }) 13... g6 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 14. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:11] guarding the d pawn. Opening up the diagonal for the bishop. } Kg7 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 15. Qf4 { [%clk 0:02:40] Ng5 ideas. Threatening Qh6+ } Rh8 { [%clk 0:03:04] } 16. Ng5 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Bxg5?? { [%clk 0:03:02] blunders mate in one. } 17. Qxf7# { 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

