{ https://youtu.be/o5ICyFPmOPg?si=jCLCn84_U9IobqV5&t=9 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 4. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:52] preventing Ne4 } (4. f4 c5 5. c3 Nc6 6. Nf3 Bd6 7. O-O cxd4 8. exd4 Ne4 (8... O-O 9. Qe2 Ne7 10. Ne5 Ne4 11. Bxe4 dxe4 12. Qxe4 { If our opponent delays Ne4 there is still a chance to prevent it with Qe2. }) 9. Ne5 f5 { In this line we don't need to play g3 but Aman points out we will be allowing our opponent Ne4. It's still objectively good for white, but Aman says it's "annoying" because the opponent is playing the SW, not us. He explains in the post game how you have to pick your poison--g3 or else face the SW. }) 4... c5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 5. c3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 6. f4 { [%clk 0:04:49] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:42] } (6... Bd6 7. Ngf3 cxd4 8. cxd4 { This would have been better from the opponent. Forcing us to take with our c pawn or face losing the f pawn. } b6 9. a3 { Aman points out in the post game analysis that we likely want to play a3 in the case we are forced to play cxd4 so that we can get some control over the b4 square and keep the knight out. }) 7. exd4 { [%clk 0:04:16] Taking back our favored way but we will be forced to weaken the kingside pawns on Bd6 } Bd6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 8. g3 { [%clk 0:04:12] covering f4 } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:22] } 9. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:10] } a6 { [%clk 0:04:18] } 10. O-O { [%clk 0:04:08] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:10] } 11. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:05] } Ne8 { [%clk 0:03:58] likely intending f6 or f5. } 12. Qf3 { [%clk 0:02:58] My instinct was Qe1. It's often an idea to put it here to eye h4 but also support the e pawn push/ or keep Ne4 from happening. However, in this case there is no e pawn and no Ne4 threat so Qf3 seems more active and also has the benefit of possibly supporting a g pawn push in the future. Either way engine evaluates both as about equal.
Another thing worth noting is that the g pawn here blocks the queen's route to the h file in this case if we play Qe1. } (12. Qe1) 12... f6 { [%clk 0:03:55] } 13. Qh5 { [%clk 0:02:41] Threatening mate. Aman expects f5 and for the opponent to route their knight to e4 which we now can't prevent.
He also notes one downside of our earlier g3 push--the DSB maneuver no longer works. So we are going to need to reroute our knight and move our rooks across after playing something like Be3 in order to create attacking chances. } f5 { [%clk 0:02:27] } (13... fxe5 14. Bxh7+ (14. Qxh7+ { This would be a mistake. Allows the king to escape! } Kf7 15. fxe5+ Ke7 16. exd6+ Kxd6 17. Rxf8 Kc7 { Still, wouldn't be a terrible position. }) 14... Kh8 15. Bg6+ Kg8 16. Qh7# { A great way to get mated }) (13... g6 14. Nxg6 hxg6 15. Qxg6+ Ng7 16. Qh7+ Kf7 17. Bg6+ Ke7 18. Qxg7+ Rf7 19. Qxf7# { Attacking the queen not an option. }) 14. Ndf3 { [%clk 0:02:37] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:02:25] } 15. Qh3 { [%clk 0:02:33] } Bxe5 { [%clk 0:02:18] } 16. fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:20] avoiding the knight trade. Aman thinks our knight threatening g5 is an asset we don't want to trade. } Ne4 { [%clk 0:02:12] } 17. g4 { [%clk 0:02:11] thematic g4 push. Well supported by the queen. Opens up the diagonal for the DSB maneuver, but Aman still plans Be3. The primary idea perhaps Kh1... Rg1? } fxg4 { [%clk 0:02:06] Aman is surprised by the capture. (loosens the knight) } 18. Qxg4 { [%clk 0:02:07] } Ne7 { [%clk 0:01:53] Preventing Bh6 ideas. } 19. Bf4 { [%clk 0:01:29] Aman considered Ng5, but felt it was a bad habit to expose the rook on the open file without the rooks being connected. } (19. Bh6 Nf5 { Black is winning here. }) 19... Ng6 { [%clk 0:01:48] Aman not impressed. He thought the knight belonged on f5 } 20. Be3 { [%clk 0:01:23] } Bc6?? { [%clk 0:01:32] hangs the e pawn with check. } 21. Qxe6+ { [%clk 0:01:06] } Kh8 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 22. Qg4 { [%clk 0:01:05] pick his pocket and get out. } Qe7 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 23. Ng5 { [%clk 0:00:37] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 24. Qh5 { [%clk 0:00:30] } Nxg5 { [%clk 0:01:01] } 25. Bxg5 { [%clk 0:00:20] Aman elects to "keep it simple" } (25. Bxg6 { This was stronger }) 25... Qe8 { [%clk 0:00:57] } 26. Rxf8+ { [%clk 0:00:17] } Qxf8?? { [%clk 0:00:54] hangs the knight. (defending pawn is pinned) } (26... Nxf8 { this was needed. }) 27. Bxg6 { [%clk 0:00:15] } h6 { [%clk 0:00:53] } 28. Rf1 { [%clk 0:00:13] } Qg8 { [%clk 0:00:50] } 29. Bxh6 { [%clk 0:00:12] } Bg4 { [%clk 0:00:44] } 30. Qh4 { [%clk 0:00:07] } gxh6 { [%clk 0:00:44] } 31. Qxh6+ { [%clk 0:00:06] } Qh7 { [%clk 0:00:40] } 32. Rf8+ { [%clk 0:00:04] when you have mate in 1, look for better } Rxf8 { [%clk 0:00:37] } 33. Qxf8+ { [%clk 0:00:03] when you have mate in 1, look for better } Qg8 { [%clk 0:00:35] } 34. Qh6+ { [%clk 0:00:03] } Qh7 { [%clk 0:00:35] } 35. Qxh7# { [%clk 0:00:02] 1-0 White wins. } 1-0

