{ https://youtu.be/jzAMNO9Z6KU?si=_B1w_uy8PAnPs3i2&t=2845 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } a6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:54] Again with the early f4. We usually play this when there is a threat of e5 but there is not here. Again with the a6 and early f4. See this game: https://lichess.org/study/Tk9U1pMu/wiK7L4sc. We want to respond to Bg4 with Nf3. } (4. Nd2 Bg4 5. f3 Bh5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Qb3 Rb8 8. f4 e6 9. Ngf3 Bd6 10. O-O O-O { Perhaps Aman is concerned about Bg4 being annoying, hence the quick f4? He doesn't explain in the video. }) 4... e6 { [%clk 0:04:31] We are pleased to see our opponent block in his LSB } 5. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:52] } g6 { [%clk 0:04:30] } 6. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:50] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:28] } 7. c3 { [%clk 0:04:49] stopping Nb4 } Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:26] } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:48] } Ng4 { [%clk 0:04:24] } 9. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:36] } Nh6?! { [%clk 0:04:16] } 10. e4 { [%clk 0:04:22] getting in e4 before Ne5. Presumably because Ne5 could be met with f6 } (10. Ne5 f6 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. e4 f5 13. e5 { This would also be fine for us. }) 10... dxe4 { [%clk 0:04:14] } 11. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:04:21] } Nf5 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 12. Ne5 { [%clk 0:04:19] } O-O { [%clk 0:03:58] } 13. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:09] } Qh4 { [%clk 0:03:48] } 14. g3 { [%clk 0:03:58] } Qh3? { [%clk 0:03:43] } 15. Nf2 { [%clk 0:03:40] Aman thinks our opponent is losing a piece here. } Qh6 { [%clk 0:03:41] } (15... Qh5 { Seems that Aman expected this for some reason. } 16. g4 { pawn fork. } Qh4 17. gxf5 { Seems this is the piece Aman was thinking of. } exf5) 16. Be4 { [%clk 0:02:36] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 17. fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:34] Discovering an attack on the queen. } g5! { [%clk 0:03:27] } 18. Qg4 { [%clk 0:01:48] } f6 { [%clk 0:03:22] } 19. exf6 { [%clk 0:01:39] } Bxf6 { [%clk 0:03:20] } 20. Nh3 { [%clk 0:01:27] } Kh8 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 21. Bxg5 { [%clk 0:01:24] } Rg8 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 22. Bxh6 { [%clk 0:01:00] } Rxg4 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 23. Bf4 { [%clk 0:00:54] best move according to the engine, keeping pieces on the board. Aman was also tempted by Bxf5. } Kg7 { [%clk 0:02:47] } 24. Nf2 { [%clk 0:00:43] } Rg6 { [%clk 0:02:39] } 25. g4 { [%clk 0:00:42] pawn is pinned so we are not yet threatening the knight. } Kf8 { [%clk 0:02:27] } 26. Kh1 { [%clk 0:00:40] Unpinning the pawn and threatening the knight. } Nd6? { [%clk 0:02:02] } 27. Bxd6+ { [%clk 0:00:39] } cxd6 { [%clk 0:01:59] } 28. Bxg6 { [%clk 0:00:38] } hxg6 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 29. Ne4 { [%clk 0:00:38] } b5 { [%clk 0:01:51] } 30. Rxf6+ { [%clk 0:00:28] The prophecy is finally fulfilled. } Ke7 { [%clk 0:01:46] } 31. Raf1 { [%clk 0:00:28] } e5 { [%clk 0:01:33] } 32. d5 { [%clk 0:00:27] taking away the king's escape square. } Bxg4 { [%clk 0:01:28] } 33. Rf7+ { [%clk 0:00:26] } Kd8 { [%clk 0:01:22] } 34. Rf8+ { [%clk 0:00:23] } Kc7 { [%clk 0:01:22] } 35. R1f7+ { [%clk 0:00:21] } Kb6 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 36. Rxa8 { [%clk 0:00:20] } Bf5 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 37. Nxd6 { [%clk 0:00:11] } Be4+?? { [%clk 0:01:14] } 38. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:00:10] } g5 { [%clk 0:01:11] } 39. b4 { [%clk 0:00:06] setting up mate in one. } a5 { [%clk 0:01:04] } 40. Nc5 { [%clk 0:00:04] when you have mate in one, look for better. } axb4 { [%clk 0:01:00] } 41. cxb4 { [%clk 0:00:04] } e4 { [%clk 0:00:58] } 42. Rfa7 { [%clk 0:00:04] when you have mate in one, look for better. } e3 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 43. Nd7# { [%clk 0:00:03] checkmate with a knight. } 1-0

