{ https://youtu.be/UFXz-bgTRn0?si=hftpcdd26pxqgc6U&t=12 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. e3 { [%clk 0:04:59] } g6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } Bg7 { [%clk 0:04:57] Threatening e5 } 4. f4 { [%clk 0:04:57] stopping e5 } f5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } c6 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 6. O-O { [%clk 0:04:50] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 7. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:48] Whenever we face a symmetrical SW, it is important to use ideas like our DSB maneuver to get an edge/ positive imbalances for us. } (7. Nbd2 { Don't waste time with Nd2 here. We can't keep the knight out of e4 anyway. }) 7... Ngf6 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 8. Be1 { [%clk 0:04:47] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:50] } 9. Bh4 { [%clk 0:04:44] } Re8 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 10. c3 { [%clk 0:04:40] } a5 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 11. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:38] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 12. Rb1 { [%clk 0:04:32] Defending b2 } e6 { [%clk 0:04:46] } 13. h3 { [%clk 0:04:27] Ideas like Qh2... Rg1... g4 are needed here to break through. } c5 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 14. Kh2 { [%clk 0:04:11] } a4 { [%clk 0:04:35] } 15. a3 { [%clk 0:04:08] not sure why Aman reacts to this. } (15. Ne5 a3 16. bxa3 Qc7 { Not sure why Aman thought playing a3 was so important here. }) 15... c4 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 16. Bc2 { [%clk 0:04:06] } Nf8 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 17. Bxa4 { [%clk 0:03:54] free pawn } Re7 { [%clk 0:04:31] } 18. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:48] } Ne4?? { [%clk 0:04:29] 1-0 White wins. Leaving the rook hanging. } (18... Rc7 19. Bc2 Bd7 20. g4 Ne4 21. Nxe4 dxe4 22. b3 Bxe5 23. fxe5 Qa5 24. gxf5 exf5 { We need pawn breaks to break through in the position. Typically c4 or g4. Aman explained in the post match analysis that he likes g4 because once pawns are exchanged our opponent loses there e pawn and it gives us a protected passed pawn on e5. }) 19. Nxe4 { Aman would have taken the knight. } dxe4 20. Bxe7 1-0

